.TH HV_KVP_DAEMON 8 2026-09-21 "Linux 7.0.9" "System Manager's Manual"
.SH NAME
hv_kvp_daemon \- Hyper-V guest key-value pair (KVP) exchange daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hv_kvp_daemon
.RB [ \-n ]
.RB [ \-d ]
.br
.B hv_kvp_daemon
.B \-h
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hv_kvp_daemon
is the user-space side of the Hyper-V key-value pair (KVP) exchange
service in a Linux guest.
The kernel's Hyper-V utilities driver passes requests from the host to
the daemon through the character device
.IR \%/dev/\:vmbus/\:hv_kvp ;
the daemon services each request and writes the reply back to the
device.
.PP
At startup the daemon detaches from the terminal with
.BR daemon (3)
unless
.B \-n
is given; the working directory is not changed, and standard input,
standard output and standard error are redirected to
.IR /dev/null .
It opens the system log, records operating system information and the
system's fully qualified domain name, creates
.I \%/var/\:lib/\:hyperv
(mode 0755) if it does not exist, and opens or creates the pool files
in that directory.
It then opens
.IR \%/dev/\:vmbus/\:hv_kvp ,
registers with the kernel, and serves requests until it is killed.
If a read from or a write to the device fails, for example across
hibernation, the daemon closes the device, reopens it and registers
again.
.SS Pools
The host stores, retrieves, deletes and enumerates keys in five
numbered pools.
Each pool is kept in the file
.IR \%/var/\:lib/\:hyperv/\:.kvp_pool_ N,
where
.I N
is the pool number, 0 to 4.
The daemon rereads a pool file before every operation on it, holding a
write lock on the whole file
.RB ( fcntl (2)
.BR F_SETLKW )
while it reads or writes the file.
A key may be at most 512 bytes and a value at most 2048 bytes; requests
to store larger keys or values fail.
.PP
Enumeration of pool 2
.RB \%(KVP_POOL_AUTO )
is not served from the pool file.
Instead the daemon answers with these keys, which it generates itself:
.TP
.B \%FullyQualifiedDomainName
The canonical name that
.BR getaddrinfo (3)
returns for the name given by
.BR gethostname (2),
looked up for IPv4 only.
It is determined once, at startup; if the lookup fails, the value is
an error message beginning
.BR "getaddrinfo failed" .
.TP
.B \%IntegrationServicesVersion
The version string that the kernel driver sends when it registers with
the daemon.
.TP
.B \%NetworkAddressIPv4
The IPv4 addresses of all interfaces other than loopback, separated by
semicolons.
.TP
.B \%NetworkAddressIPv6
The IPv6 addresses of all interfaces other than loopback, separated by
semicolons.
.TP
.B \%OSBuildNumber
The kernel release, as printed by
.BR "uname \-r" .
.TP
.B \%OSVersion
The kernel release, truncated before its first hyphen.
.TP
.B \%OSName
The
.B NAME
field of
.IR \%/etc/\:os\-release .
If that file does not exist, the first line of
.I \%/etc/\:SuSE\-release
or, failing that,
.IR \%/etc/\:redhat\-release .
Otherwise the kernel name, as printed by
.BR "uname \-s" .
.TP
.B \%OSMajorVersion
The
.B \%VERSION_ID
field of
.IR \%/etc/\:os\-release ,
or the second line of the fallback file.
Empty if neither is available.
.TP
.B \%OSMinorVersion
The third line of the fallback file; otherwise empty.
.TP
.B \%ProcessorArchitecture
The machine hardware name, as printed by
.BR "uname \-m" .
.SS Network configuration
The host can also read and set the IP configuration of a network
adapter.
.PP
To read it, the host names the adapter by its MAC address.
The daemon looks through
.I \%/sys/\:class/\:net/\:*/\:address
for interfaces with that address (ignoring case), skips any that have
no IP address, and reports for the first one it finds:
its IPv4 and IPv6 addresses and subnet masks;
the gateways of all default routes on the system, taken from the output
of
.B \%ip \-\-oneline \-4 route show
and
.BR "\%ip \-\-oneline \-6 route show" ;
the DNS servers printed by the
.B \%hv_get_dns_info
helper; and whether DHCP is enabled, according to the
.B \%hv_get_dhcp_info
helper.
.PP
To set it, the host names the adapter by the GUID of its VMBus device,
which the daemon matches against
.IR \%/sys/\:class/\:net/\:*/\:device/\:device_id .
The daemon writes the requested configuration for interface
.I \%ifname
to two files, then runs the
.B \%hv_set_ifconfig
helper with the two file names as arguments.
If the helper exits with a non-zero status, the host is told that the
request failed.
The two files are:
.TP
.IR \%/var/\:lib/\:hyperv/\:ifcfg\- ifname
.I KEY=value
lines in ifcfg format:
.BR HWADDR ,
.BR DEVICE ,
.B BOOTPROTO
.RB ( dhcp " or " none ),
.BI IPADDR n\fR,
.BI NETMASK n\fR,
.BR GATEWAY ,
.BI GATEWAY n\fR,
and
.BI DNS n\fR;
IPv6 entries use
.BR IPV6ADDR ,
.B \%IPV6NETMASK
and
.BR \%IPV6_DEFAULTGW ,
with suffixes
.BI _ n
after the first.
.TP
.IR \%/var/\:lib/\:hyperv/ ifname .nmconnection
A NetworkManager keyfile with a
.B [connection]
section
.RB \%(interface\-name ),
an
.B [ethernet]
section
.RB \%(mac\-address )
and
.B [ipv4]
and
.B [ipv6]
sections holding
.BI address n\fR=\fIaddress\fB/\fIprefix\fR,
.BR method ,
and comma-separated
.B gateway
and
.B dns
lists.
In
.BR [ipv4] ,
.B method
is
.B auto
if DHCP is requested,
.B manual
if IPv4 addresses are given, and
.B disabled
otherwise.
In
.BR [ipv6] ,
.B method
is
.B manual
if IPv6 addresses are given, and
.B auto
otherwise.
.SS Helper programs
The helpers are in
.IR \%/usr/\:lib/\:x86_64\-linux\-peios/\:hypervkvpd/ .
The daemon runs each one through the shell and reads its standard
output:
.TP
.BI \%hv_get_dns_info " ifname"
Prints the addresses of the DNS servers, one per line.
The installed script prints the
.B nameserver
entries of
.I \%/etc/\:resolv.conf
and ignores
.IR \%ifname .
.TP
.BI \%hv_get_dhcp_info " ifname"
Prints output beginning with
.B Enabled
if DHCP is enabled on
.IR \%ifname ;
any other output means that it is not.
The installed script prints
.B Enabled
if
.IR \%/etc/\:sysconfig/\:network\-scripts/\:ifcfg\- ifname
contains the string
.BR dhcp ,
and
.B Disabled
otherwise.
.TP
.BI \%hv_set_ifconfig " ifcfg-file keyfile"
Applies the configuration in the two files described above.
The installed script appends
.BR \%IPV6INIT=yes ,
.BR \%NM_CONTROLLED=no ,
.B \%PEERDNS=yes
and
.B \%ONBOOT=yes
to
.I \%ifcfg\-file
and copies it to
.IR \%/etc/\:sysconfig/\:network\-scripts/ ,
writes
.I keyfile
with
.B \%autoconnect=true
added to its
.B [connection]
section into
.IR \%/etc/\:NetworkManager/\:system\-connections/ ,
and then runs
.B \%/sbin/\:ifdown
and
.BR \%/sbin/\:ifup .
.PP
The installed helpers are the example scripts from the Linux source
tree, unmodified.
Their comments describe them as examples that each distribution is
expected to implement for its own network configuration;
.B \%hv_set_ifconfig
is written for a RHEL-style system.
Replacing the files in the helper directory changes how the daemon
gathers and applies network configuration.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-no\-daemon
Stay in the foreground; do not detach from the terminal.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-debug
Log debug messages at priority
.BR \%LOG_DEBUG :
the contents of every pool at startup, and each key that is added,
updated or deleted.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary to standard error and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B hv_kvp_daemon
exits with status 0 after
.BR \-\-help ,
and with status 1 for an invalid option, if
.BR daemon (3)
fails, or on a fatal error such as failing to open
.I \%/dev/\:vmbus/\:hv_kvp
or to register with the kernel.
When the daemon detaches, the invoking process exits with status 0 as
soon as the background process has been created; later errors are
reported only in the system log.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I \%/dev/\:vmbus/\:hv_kvp
KVP device, provided by the kernel's Hyper-V utilities driver.
.TP
.I \%/var/\:lib/\:hyperv/
Directory holding the pool files and the generated network
configuration files.
.TP
.IR \%/var/\:lib/\:hyperv/\:.kvp_pool_0 " to " \%.kvp_pool_4
Pool files, created with mode 0644.
Each is a sequence of fixed-size records: a 512-byte key followed by a
2048-byte value.
The pool numbers are defined in
.I \%include/\:uapi/\:linux/\:hyperv.h
in the Linux source: 0
.RB \%(KVP_POOL_EXTERNAL ),
1
.RB \%(KVP_POOL_GUEST ),
2
.RB \%(KVP_POOL_AUTO ),
3
.RB \%(KVP_POOL_AUTO_EXTERNAL )
and 4
.RB \%(KVP_POOL_AUTO_INTERNAL ).
.TP
.IR \%/var/\:lib/\:hyperv/\:ifcfg\- ifname
.TQ
.IR \%/var/\:lib/\:hyperv/ ifname .nmconnection
Network configuration written when the host sets an adapter's IP
configuration.
.TP
.I \%/usr/\:lib/\:x86_64\-linux\-peios/\:hypervkvpd/
Helper programs
.BR \%hv_get_dhcp_info ,
.B \%hv_get_dns_info
and
.BR \%hv_set_ifconfig .
.TP
.I \%/etc/\:os\-release
.TQ
.I \%/etc/\:SuSE\-release
.TQ
.I \%/etc/\:redhat\-release
Sources of the operating system name and version.
.TP
.I \%/sys/\:class/\:net/
Used to map MAC addresses and VMBus device GUIDs to interface names.
.SH NOTES
The daemon logs through
.BR syslog (3)
with the identifier
.B KVP
and the facility
.BR \%LOG_USER .
.PP
The daemon works only in a Linux guest running on Hyper-V.
.I \%/dev/\:vmbus/\:hv_kvp
is created by the kernel's Hyper-V utilities driver, hv_utils
.RB \%(CONFIG_HYPERV_UTILS );
if the device cannot be opened, the daemon exits.
.PP
The fully qualified domain name is determined once, at startup;
the daemon does not see later changes to the system's host name.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR \%hv_fcopy_uio_daemon (8),
.BR \%hv_vss_daemon (8),
.BR lsvmbus (1),
.BR daemon (3),
.BR syslog (3)
.PP
.I \%Documentation/\:virt/\:hyperv/
in the Linux source.
